= V1 to V2 maturation =

BITwiki V2 is not a rewrite of V1. It is the maturation of a system whose design imagination was substantially larger than its deployed MediaWiki implementation.

V1 explored sophisticated ontology, epistemics, confidence trails, templates, schemas, modules, semantic relationships, portals, transclusion, knowledge graphs, progressive disclosure, recursive refinement, and human–AI knowledge production. The archived public implementation proves that only part of this architecture became live wiki machinery.

That difference is not a defect to erase. It is one of the most important forms of V1 evidence.

<blockquote>'''V1 gave us the imagination. V2 gives that imagination a coherent substrate.'''</blockquote>

== Governing principle ==

<pre>
V1
→ preserve
→ understand
→ identify signal
→ mature primitive
→ enhance
→ integrate into V2
</pre>

The objective is harmony, not replacement.

V2 should preserve what made V1 distinctive—its systems orientation, experimental ambition, crypto-punk independence, epistemic skepticism, composability, recursive research character, and unusual authored voice—while making the underlying architecture more coherent, explicit, testable, and usable.

'''Preserve the vision. Preserve the voice. Preserve the experiments. Improve the machinery.'''

== Two V1 evidence layers ==

Every migration decision must distinguish at least two historical layers.

=== V1 design imagination ===
Design pages, manifestos, charters, architecture specifications, wanted templates, schema proposals, ontology writings, Book Matter research, and other authored material record what BITwiki was attempting to become.

These sources are evidence of intent, conceptual invention, worldview, vocabulary, and desired capability. They remain valuable even when no corresponding namespace object or runtime capability existed.

=== V1 implementation reality ===
The archived MediaWiki corpus records what was actually instantiated and used: pages, revisions, templates, category memberships, properties, imported vocabularies, MediaWiki configuration, transclusions, redirects, files, and maintenance state.

Implementation evidence determines what V1 actually did. Design evidence determines what V1 was trying to do.

Neither layer should impersonate the other.

See [[BITwiki:V1 implementation audit]] and [[BITwiki:Source lineage]].

== Maturation test ==

A V1 idea should not become a V2 feature merely because it was documented. Before promotion, ask:

# '''What was the original intellectual or operational job?'''
# '''What evidence shows how V1 attempted to perform that job?'''
# '''Did the idea survive use, revision, contradiction, or later ecosystem development?'''
# '''Is the job already performed by a native MediaWiki primitive, existing V2 property, template, relation, Book Matter unit, or navigation view?'''
# '''What is the smallest mature primitive that performs the job without recreating V1 conflation?'''
# '''Can it scale compositionally without forcing every page into unnecessary machinery?'''
# '''Can humans understand it and machines query or reuse it?'''
# '''Can its behavior be validated?'''

The result may be preservation, direct continuation, refactoring, decomposition, implementation, historical retention, or retirement. Retirement of a mechanism does not imply erasure of the idea that produced it.

